        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Blood pressure is measured using two numbers: systolic (the top number measuring pressure when your heart beats) and diastolic (the bottom number measuring pressure when your heart rests between beats). While the ideal medical target remains under 120/80 mm Hg for adults, average blood pressure readings naturally increase as you get older because blood vessels and arteries become stiffer over time.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Average Blood Pressure Ranges by Adult Age Group"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Ages 18–39: Women average about 110/68 mm Hg; men average about 119/70 mm Hg."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Ages 40–59: Women average about 122/74 mm Hg; men average about 124/77 mm Hg."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Ages 60 and older: Women average about 139/68 mm Hg; men average about 133/69 mm Hg."
          }
        ],
